From: PDF-XChange AUR maintainer workaround
Subject: rpcrt4: wait on named-pipe listeners in chunks to avoid the 64-handle limit

WaitForMultipleObjectsEx() only accepts up to MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS (64)
handles.  Some applications, notably PDF-XChange Editor's in-process COM
server (and Steam), keep more than 63 named-pipe listeners registered on a
single protseq.  Once the wait array grows past 64 entries the wait fails
with error 87 (STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER):

    err:rpc:rpcrt4_protseq_np_wait_for_new_connection wait failed with error 87

which makes RPCRT4_server_thread() shut down, leaving the process's COM
marshaling deadlocked:

    err:sync:RtlpWaitForCriticalSection ... rpc_server.c: server_cs ... blocked by ...
    err:sync:RtlpWaitForCriticalSection ... combase/rpc.c: dcom registered server interfaces ... blocked by ...
    err:seh:call_seh_handlers invalid frame ...
    err:seh:NtRaiseException Exception frame is not in stack limits ...

This is Wine bug 55705 (related: 60334).  Work around the limit by waiting
on the listener handles in chunks of at most 64 and cycling through all of
them until one is signaled, instead of letting the wait overflow.

The hunk below was generated against Wine master
(dlls/rpcrt4/rpc_transport.c, rpcrt4_protseq_np_wait_for_new_connection).
The surrounding comment block is stable across Wine 8/9/10/11, so the patch
usually applies with fuzz on those trees; if it does not apply, hand-apply
the change shown below (it is the only difference).

--- a/dlls/rpcrt4/rpc_transport.c	2026-10-11 16:20:26.456577633 +0200
+++ b/dlls/rpcrt4/rpc_transport.c	2026-10-11 16:20:26.488727201 +0200
@@ -727,7 +727,34 @@
          * overlapped I/O implementation in Wine we need to free some memory
          * by the file user APC being called, even if no completion routine was
          * specified at the time of starting the async operation */
-        res = WaitForMultipleObjectsEx(count, objs, FALSE, INFINITE, TRUE);
+        if (count > MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS)
+        {
+            /* WaitForMultipleObjectsEx only accepts up to MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS
+             * (64) handles.  Some applications (PDF-XChange Editor, Steam)
+             * accumulate more than 63 listeners on one protseq; once the wait
+             * array grows past 64 entries the wait fails with error 87
+             * (STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER) and the RPC server thread shuts
+             * down, leaving the whole process's COM marshaling deadlocked
+             * (Wine bugs #55705, #60334).  Wait in chunks instead, cycling
+             * through all listeners until one is signaled. */
+            res = WAIT_TIMEOUT;
+            while (res == WAIT_TIMEOUT)
+            {
+                unsigned int offset, chunk;
+                for (offset = 0; offset < count && res == WAIT_TIMEOUT; offset += chunk)
+                {
+                    chunk = min(MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS, count - offset);
+                    res = WaitForMultipleObjectsEx(chunk, &objs[offset], FALSE, 1000, TRUE);
+                    if (res == WAIT_IO_COMPLETION) break;
+                    if (res >= WAIT_OBJECT_0 && res < WAIT_OBJECT_0 + chunk)
+                        res += offset;  /* make it an index into the full array */
+                    else if (res != WAIT_TIMEOUT && res != WAIT_FAILED)
+                        res = WAIT_FAILED;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        else
+            res = WaitForMultipleObjectsEx(count, objs, FALSE, INFINITE, TRUE);
     } while (res == WAIT_IO_COMPLETION);
 
     if (res == WAIT_OBJECT_0)
